WEBVTT

00:02.000 --> 00:05.075
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:05.412 --> 00:08.753
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.815 --> 00:11.873
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:12.488 --> 00:15.396
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:15.464 --> 00:19.341
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:19.426 --> 00:22.300
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:22.351 --> 00:23.888
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.956 --> 00:25.275
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:25.343 --> 00:29.861
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.293 --> 00:37.298
Okay, Brian,
I'm goin' to the IGA.

00:37.360 --> 00:38.998
Don't let Peter
get into the cookie dough.

00:39.066 --> 00:40.736
Yeah, sure.

00:45.719 --> 00:47.072
Uh, Brian?

00:47.140 --> 00:49.212
I'm just gonna go
into the kitchen and...

00:49.279 --> 00:50.416
look at somethin'.

00:50.482 --> 00:51.620
Peter, I know
what you're doing.

00:51.688 --> 00:55.230
I'm just gonna
look at somethin'.

00:55.298 --> 00:58.892
(muffled): Oh! What a very
interesting thing.

00:58.959 --> 01:01.348
Brian, can I give you money
and you write me a check

01:01.434 --> 01:02.618
so I can order something from

01:02.687 --> 01:04.241
this Abercrombie & Fitch
catalog?

01:04.309 --> 01:05.946
What do you want to order?
Oh, wait, let me guess.

01:06.014 --> 01:07.618
Him.
(guffaws)

01:07.685 --> 01:09.189
He got you, Stewie!

01:09.258 --> 01:10.910
Well, um, if
it were you...

01:10.979 --> 01:13.987
You-you would take the magazine
and you'd put it on the floor

01:14.055 --> 01:16.896
and pee on it 'cause you're
a dog and you're stupid,

01:16.965 --> 01:20.073
and-and you have a weird
toenail halfway up your elbow.

01:20.158 --> 01:21.962
Nice comeback.

01:22.031 --> 01:23.684
Geez, that one
was all over the place.

01:23.752 --> 01:25.054
I should have been
quicker than that.

01:25.122 --> 01:26.292
I should've said, "Really?

01:26.360 --> 01:27.963
Why would I order
your ex-boyfriend?"

01:28.031 --> 01:29.368
And then I'd go like this.
(snaps fingers)

01:29.435 --> 01:30.772
Aw, that's so good!

01:30.840 --> 01:32.343
Why didn't I say that?!

01:32.411 --> 01:33.898
If only I had a time machine,

01:33.966 --> 01:35.720
I could go back in time
and have that comeback ready.

01:35.788 --> 01:36.890
Wait a minute.

01:36.959 --> 01:38.296
I do have a time machine!

01:48.762 --> 01:51.468
Brian, can I give you money

01:51.554 --> 01:53.057
and you write me a check
so I can order something

01:53.126 --> 01:54.813
from this Abercrombie
& Fitch catalog?

01:54.881 --> 01:56.634
What do you want to order?
Oh, wait, let me guess:

01:56.702 --> 01:57.939
Him.
Really?

01:58.006 --> 01:59.844
Why would I order
your ex-boyfriend?

01:59.912 --> 02:02.653
(snaps fingers) (guffaws)
He got you, Brian!

02:02.704 --> 02:04.458
(groans)

02:04.510 --> 02:06.197
Why didn't I think
of this before?

02:06.265 --> 02:08.153
Now I can always have
the last word.

02:08.221 --> 02:10.862
Like that ground control guy
during the moon landing.

02:10.930 --> 02:13.671
(over radio):
That's one small step for man,

02:13.738 --> 02:16.144
one giant leap for mankind.

02:16.212 --> 02:17.716
MISSION CONTROLLER:
Roger that, Neil.

02:17.785 --> 02:20.892
ARMSTRONG: We came in peace for
all the peoples of the Earth.

02:20.961 --> 02:22.798
MISSION CONTROLLER:
We read you, Neil.

02:22.866 --> 02:24.236
ARMSTRONG:
We chose to go to the moon,

02:24.303 --> 02:27.578
not because it is easy,
but because it is hard.

02:27.647 --> 02:29.218
MISSION CONTROLLER: Had a lot of
help down here, Neil.

02:29.286 --> 02:31.424
ARMSTRONG: We have slipped
the surly bonds of Earth

02:31.492 --> 02:33.597
and touched the face of God.

02:33.666 --> 02:34.835
MISSION CONTROLLER:
Yeah, if you could just

02:34.903 --> 02:36.741
grab some rocks, Neil,
throw 'em in a bag,

02:36.809 --> 02:38.011
we'll get you home
safe and sound.

02:40.955 --> 02:42.759
This is silly fun.

02:42.827 --> 02:45.667
I can go back in time and change
anything in Brian's life.

02:49.180 --> 02:50.785
Aw, don't feel
bad, Peter.

02:50.852 --> 02:52.522
Hey, I know what'll
cheer you up.

02:54.144 --> 02:55.850
Hey, where the hell's
my banana thing?

02:55.917 --> 02:57.722
♪ It's peanut butter
jelly time ♪

02:57.790 --> 02:59.510
♪ Peanut butter
jelly time ♪

02:59.578 --> 03:00.966
♪ Peanut butter
jelly time ♪

03:01.033 --> 03:02.586
♪ Where he at?
Where he at? ♪

03:02.654 --> 03:05.195
(laughing):
Oh... Oh, my God.

03:05.262 --> 03:07.669
Oh, Stewie, that
is so funny!

03:07.736 --> 03:10.210
Oh... I did not
see that comin'!

03:10.277 --> 03:12.132
But... that was my thing.

03:12.200 --> 03:14.239
I'm pretty sure it was
the Internet's thing.

03:14.307 --> 03:15.777
♪ Peanut butter jelly,
peanut butter jelly ♪

03:15.845 --> 03:17.483
♪ Peanut butter jelly
with a baseball bat. ♪

03:20.342 --> 03:22.932
Hey, you remember
a few months ago

03:22.999 --> 03:25.675
when you scored that date with
that Victoria's Secret model

03:25.742 --> 03:27.044
and then ended up
sealing the deal?

03:27.111 --> 03:28.616
Oh, yeah. That was amazing.

03:28.684 --> 03:29.820
What was the date of that?

03:29.888 --> 03:33.079
August 15th. August 15th!
You betcha!

03:37.510 --> 03:39.048
Now, I have to
leave enough time...

03:39.115 --> 03:40.100
Talking to yourself?

03:40.169 --> 03:42.442
Gyah...! Brian, Brian!
Hello!

03:42.510 --> 03:45.066
Stewie, I know you've been going
back in time to embarrass me.

03:45.134 --> 03:48.159
Oh, what-what,
that... come on!

03:48.227 --> 03:49.663
Oh, okay, then-then
where are you going now?

03:49.749 --> 03:52.087
Well, I have a-a-a very
important mission.

03:52.155 --> 03:53.843
Uh-huh. Let me see that panel.
No, no, don't touch that!

03:53.928 --> 03:54.930
Come on, let me see that!
Don't...

03:54.997 --> 03:56.567
Move out of the way!
No, you can't...

03:56.635 --> 03:57.520
I'm not gonna let you do this...
You just touched my boob.

03:57.606 --> 03:58.775
I'm gonna tell Mom.

03:58.842 --> 04:01.900
(beeping rapidly,
electricity crackling)

04:31.275 --> 04:34.736
What happened?

04:34.803 --> 04:36.574
Where are we?
I don't know.

04:36.642 --> 04:39.516
It's like...
we're not anywhere.

04:39.600 --> 04:41.187
Stewie, what the hell
did your machine do?!

04:41.272 --> 04:42.776
Just calm down,
calm down, Brian.

04:42.827 --> 04:45.535
I've got the return pad.

04:45.603 --> 04:46.922
All right, step on.

04:48.010 --> 04:50.047
What happened?

04:50.115 --> 04:51.804
Good Lord!

04:51.872 --> 04:53.459
This is impossible!

04:53.527 --> 04:55.565
What? Well, the return pad works

04:55.650 --> 04:57.488
by first locking onto
our coordinates

04:57.556 --> 04:59.327
along the curve of the
space-time continuum.

04:59.395 --> 05:01.550
But it can't.
Why not?

05:01.635 --> 05:03.757
Well, the only explanation is...

05:03.825 --> 05:05.696
we've somehow been transported

05:05.765 --> 05:07.569
outside the space-time
continuum.

05:07.654 --> 05:09.173
I don't-I don't understand.

05:09.242 --> 05:10.343
STEWIE:
Non-existence.

05:10.411 --> 05:12.183
No past, no future.

05:12.250 --> 05:13.838
No universe.

05:13.906 --> 05:16.111
But still
somehow a large,

05:16.179 --> 05:18.385
<i>brightly-colored promo
for The Cleveland Show.</i>

05:18.453 --> 05:21.159
<i>Hey, y'all,
it's The Cleveland Show!</i>

05:21.227 --> 05:22.497
STEWIE:
Huh. That's weird.

05:22.566 --> 05:24.403
Black guys usually
don't promote themselves.

05:28.352 --> 05:30.475
Come on, Stewie, can you
fix that damn thing or not?

05:30.543 --> 05:31.762
Well, it's not
broken, Brian.

05:31.847 --> 05:34.136
It operates according
to the laws of physics,

05:34.203 --> 05:36.627
but we're outside the
space-time continuum,

05:36.694 --> 05:38.750
<i>so there are no laws
of physics-- watch.</i>

05:42.278 --> 05:43.648
How the hell'd
you do that?

05:43.715 --> 05:44.935
No laws of physics, Brian.

05:45.003 --> 05:46.138
We create our own physics.

05:46.206 --> 05:47.259
See, watch this.

05:47.327 --> 05:49.315
(snaps fingers)

05:49.400 --> 05:51.238
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh,
bum-bah, duh-duh ♪

05:51.289 --> 05:53.544
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh,
bum-bah, duh-duh... ♪

05:53.612 --> 05:56.085
♪ Heart and soul ♪
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh,
bum-bah, duh-duh ♪

05:56.137 --> 05:57.957
♪ I fell in love with you ♪
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh,
bum-bah, duh-duh ♪

05:57.959 --> 05:59.964
♪ Heart and soul ♪
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh,
bum-bah, duh-duh ♪

05:59.966 --> 06:01.802
♪ The way a fool would do ♪
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh♪

06:01.855 --> 06:03.976
♪ Madly ♪
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh,
bum-bah, duh-duh ♪

06:03.978 --> 06:07.170
♪ Because you held me tight ♪
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh, m-bah, duh-duh ♪

06:07.255 --> 06:10.079
♪ And stole a kiss in the night ♪
♪ Bum-bah, duh-duh, bum-bah... ♪

06:10.147 --> 06:11.383
(chuckles)
Did I tell you?

06:11.468 --> 06:13.055
Yeah, that was good.
We were good.

06:13.121 --> 06:15.026
Yeah, th-that's why it
sucks that we're alone here.

06:15.094 --> 06:16.632
Because if-if-if
there were people here,

06:16.699 --> 06:18.471
<i>they would say,
"That was good.</i>

06:18.539 --> 06:19.692
<i>You guys should
do that more."</i>

06:19.760 --> 06:20.710
Oh, yeah, they totally
would, but look-look,

06:20.795 --> 06:21.748
I still want to
go home, Stewie.

06:21.833 --> 06:23.136
How do we get
outta here?

06:23.203 --> 06:25.644
Well, if I overload
the return pad's reactor,

06:25.712 --> 06:27.214
it might release enough energy

06:27.282 --> 06:29.973
to blow us back into the
universe, but it's too risky.

06:30.042 --> 06:31.276
But it could
get us home?

06:31.345 --> 06:33.416
Well, maybe, but
it'll break my thing.

06:33.484 --> 06:35.656
And if I overload that reactor,
we could both die.

06:35.725 --> 06:37.045
Well, you at least
have to try.

06:37.096 --> 06:40.639
And if you don't, I am gonna
spend the rest of eternity

06:40.723 --> 06:42.928
chuckling at a joke that
I am not gonna share with you.

06:42.980 --> 06:45.955
(chuckles)
Oh, oh, man.

06:46.039 --> 06:47.442
What? What's the joke?

06:47.511 --> 06:49.633
(laughs)
Oh, yes!

06:49.718 --> 06:52.258
What? What is it?
(laughs) You-you can't say that!

06:52.309 --> 06:54.013
But it got said!

06:54.080 --> 06:55.200
Wha-what what?!
By who?!

06:55.268 --> 06:56.739
All right, fine, fine!

06:56.806 --> 06:57.824
I'll try it,
you bastard!

06:57.892 --> 06:59.145
Now, step onto
the return pad.

06:59.198 --> 07:00.750
Now brace yourself.

07:00.819 --> 07:03.776
Hopefully, the explosion
will propel us into reality.

07:03.843 --> 07:07.604
(beep, whirring,
electricity buzzing)

07:23.572 --> 07:25.777
(electricity buzzing,
crackling)

07:29.539 --> 07:31.109
We made it.
Yeah, we made it.

07:31.178 --> 07:32.816
Okay, now tell me the joke.
Oh, it's nothing really.

07:32.883 --> 07:34.387
It's just this bit about
a planned parenthood clinic

07:34.455 --> 07:35.690
on Halloween.

07:35.758 --> 07:37.696
Next.

07:40.573 --> 07:43.079
Brian, I am about to
show you something

07:43.147 --> 07:44.819
that's gonna
blow your mind!

07:44.887 --> 07:46.859
Do you know what the
big bang theory is?

07:46.925 --> 07:48.530
Yeah, the theory that
the universe started

07:48.598 --> 07:50.201
with a massive outward explosion

07:50.269 --> 07:52.943
from a singularity of infinite
mass and infinite density.

07:53.011 --> 07:54.716
Check out the big brain
on Brian.

07:54.784 --> 07:56.638
Good. Now, take a look.

07:56.706 --> 07:59.313
STEWIE: The universe's cosmic
background radiation,

07:59.382 --> 08:01.052
a kind of echo
of the big bang,

08:01.120 --> 08:04.611
precisely matches the energy
spectrum of my return pad!

08:04.679 --> 08:06.200
Well, what does
that mean?

08:06.268 --> 08:09.578
<i>It means my return pad's
explosion was the big bang.</i>

08:09.646 --> 08:12.386
But the big bang happened, like,
billions of years ago.

08:12.454 --> 08:15.263
No, no-- we were outside
the space-time continuum.

08:15.330 --> 08:18.571
Time and space didn't
exist until my explosion.

08:18.639 --> 08:21.079
<i>Which means I created
the universe, Brian.</i>

08:21.146 --> 08:22.651
That doesn't make any sense.

08:22.718 --> 08:24.723
<i>You were born in the universe.
How could you create it?</i>

08:24.792 --> 08:26.764
It's called a "temporal
causality loop."

08:26.831 --> 08:29.505
The universe created me,
so I could create it,

08:29.573 --> 08:31.444
so it could create
me and so on.

08:31.512 --> 08:32.983
That's...

08:33.051 --> 08:35.323
that's the most incredible
thing I've ever heard.

08:35.390 --> 08:36.760
So, wait, that-that
means that...

08:36.829 --> 08:38.967
I kinda created
the universe, too.

08:39.035 --> 08:41.508
(wry laugh)
No, no, no, no.

08:41.576 --> 08:44.351
You're, you're sort of the
Art Garfunkel of the universe.

08:48.598 --> 08:51.706
Hey, Lois, check out what I
spelled with my Alpha-Bits.

08:51.791 --> 08:54.046
(laughing):
Oh, my God.

08:54.114 --> 08:56.220
Yeah! Now watch.

08:59.196 --> 09:00.885
(laughing):
Oh, maybe later,

09:00.953 --> 09:01.954
you'll want
the real thing.

09:02.022 --> 09:04.028
(both laughing)

09:04.096 --> 09:05.934
I prefer the Alpha-Bits.

09:06.002 --> 09:07.772
Well, I'm off
to the farmers' market.

09:07.841 --> 09:10.146
I've gotta pick up some
plutonium for a new return pad.

09:10.214 --> 09:12.921
In case I decide to make
another universe later.

09:12.989 --> 09:14.559
Plutonium at the
farmers' market?

09:14.627 --> 09:16.800
Yeah, I'm only using
organic plutonium now.

09:16.867 --> 09:18.606
Think globally:
buy locally.

09:18.674 --> 09:20.612
Hey, slut,
get me out of this.

09:20.679 --> 09:23.689
(crowd murmuring)

09:23.756 --> 09:25.678
Hello there, Yusuf.
Hey, Stewie!

09:25.745 --> 09:27.147
What can I get you?

09:27.216 --> 09:28.418
Oh, just a little
of the good stuff

09:28.487 --> 09:29.588
for a new time machine
return pad.

09:29.657 --> 09:31.027
Coming right up.

09:31.094 --> 09:33.366
So, how's the whole
terrorism thing goin'?

09:33.434 --> 09:36.309
Well, the good news is
my son died in a car bombing.

09:36.378 --> 09:39.134
The bad news is
he borrowed my car!

09:39.203 --> 09:43.047
♪ ♪

09:43.115 --> 09:44.586
(laughs)

09:44.653 --> 09:45.956
Oh, God!

09:46.022 --> 09:47.927
Oh, that's
the other reason I come here.

09:47.995 --> 09:50.620
Okay, so here
is your plutonium.

09:50.688 --> 09:52.894
I hope you kill
my bitch wife with it.

09:52.962 --> 09:54.734
Whoa, whoa. Where's
that comin' from?

09:54.801 --> 09:57.440
Eh, I'm just a little upset
because she left me.

09:57.508 --> 10:00.484
I guess she didn't know
what "jihad."

10:00.551 --> 10:02.222
♪ ♪

10:02.289 --> 10:03.443
Come on!
Oh, sorry!

10:03.494 --> 10:07.773
♪ ♪

10:07.840 --> 10:08.959
So...

10:09.028 --> 10:11.651
Stewie Griffin invented
a time machine.

10:37.598 --> 10:39.720
(gasps)
Bertram!

10:39.788 --> 10:40.924
Hello, Stewie.

10:40.992 --> 10:42.663
You don't realize it yet,

10:42.730 --> 10:45.370
but your life is about
to cease to exist.

10:45.439 --> 10:47.109
What are you talking about?

10:47.177 --> 10:49.885
I am going to kill
one of your ancestors,

10:49.953 --> 10:52.626
effectively erasing you
from history!

10:52.694 --> 10:54.097
Here! Play with this!

10:54.165 --> 10:56.070
At least you can spend
your final moments

10:56.137 --> 10:58.110
doing something you enjoy.

10:58.178 --> 11:00.416
You know, it's funny, I had
actually stopped playing

11:00.484 --> 11:02.255
with that toy, but now that
I see you with it,

11:02.323 --> 11:03.493
I kinda want to play
with it again.

11:03.544 --> 11:04.595
(giddy laughter)

11:04.663 --> 11:06.267
Look at me! I'm mowing the lawn!

11:06.335 --> 11:07.338
(giddy laughter)

11:07.389 --> 11:09.043
(time machine hatch shuts)

11:13.375 --> 11:15.280
What the hell's
all that noise?

11:15.348 --> 11:16.516
Brian, it was Bertram!

11:16.584 --> 11:17.720
He's gone into the past,

11:17.788 --> 11:19.091
and he claims he's
going to...

11:19.174 --> 11:20.729
(gasps)

11:20.813 --> 11:22.568
Stewie, what's going on?!

11:22.652 --> 11:23.939
(whooshing)

11:25.528 --> 11:27.751
(gasps)
Peter, what was that?!

11:29.624 --> 11:31.679
(whooshing)

11:41.125 --> 11:43.397
Quick, Brian, into
the time machine!

11:44.854 --> 11:46.375
Stewie, what the hell
was that?!

11:46.458 --> 11:48.380
Those were ripples
in time, Brian,

11:48.464 --> 11:50.654
emanating from some
point in the past.

11:50.722 --> 11:52.793
And there's only
one explanation.

11:52.861 --> 11:55.285
Bertram told me he planned
to kill one of my ancestors.

11:55.352 --> 11:57.374
He must have succeeded,

11:57.442 --> 11:59.347
thereby changing the
past and erasing... me.

11:59.415 --> 12:01.436
But what has that got
to do with...?

12:01.505 --> 12:03.427
I created the universe, Brian!

12:03.511 --> 12:05.934
Bertram didn't know that by
removing me from history,

12:06.019 --> 12:08.274
I wouldn't be there to be
accidentally thrown outside

12:08.342 --> 12:09.928
the universe and subsequently
create it.

12:09.980 --> 12:11.936
So the universe never existed!

12:12.003 --> 12:13.641
Well, we have
to stop him!

12:13.708 --> 12:16.466
Yes. Unfortunately, Bertram
took the return pad with him,

12:16.550 --> 12:18.137
so we'll only get
one shot at this.

12:18.205 --> 12:19.408
Ah, here it is.

12:19.476 --> 12:21.314
Now we'll have to
travel back in time

12:21.382 --> 12:23.588
to a point shortly before
Bertram's arrival.

12:23.657 --> 12:24.724
Get ready, Brian.

12:37.732 --> 12:39.905
How long until
Bertram arrives?

12:39.973 --> 12:42.379
I can't be certain, but
I'd say about 15 minutes.

12:42.447 --> 12:43.682
Where do you
think we are?

12:43.750 --> 12:44.869
I don't know.

12:44.938 --> 12:46.876
Looks like...
the Renaissance.

12:46.944 --> 12:48.213
It does.

12:48.281 --> 12:50.553
This doesn't count
as our trip to Italy.

12:50.622 --> 12:51.958
<i>Brian, look!
There's the Mona Lisa.</i>

12:53.196 --> 12:55.000
And it's unfinished!

12:55.069 --> 12:57.960
We must be in Leonardo da Vinci's studio!
(footsteps)

12:58.028 --> 12:59.079
Quick! Someone's coming!

13:06.772 --> 13:09.712
(gasps)
That must be Leonardo da Vinci!

13:09.780 --> 13:11.903
Stewie, he looks
just like you!

13:11.970 --> 13:13.974
Da Vinci must be
my ancestor.

13:14.043 --> 13:15.914
Good Lord!

13:15.982 --> 13:17.670
That's who Bertram's
here to kill!

13:17.737 --> 13:19.023
So that means
you're Italian.

13:19.091 --> 13:20.428
Of course!

13:20.496 --> 13:22.133
My love for SpaghettiOs
and smoking on the toilet.

13:22.201 --> 13:23.305
It all makes sense.

13:23.373 --> 13:24.407
Ew.

13:31.082 --> 13:33.188
My God, da Vinci
is your ancestor!

13:33.256 --> 13:34.609
But why would
Bertram go back

13:34.678 --> 13:36.214
this far
to get rid of you?

13:36.283 --> 13:38.154
Ah, you're forgetting I
initially encountered Bertram

13:38.222 --> 13:40.678
as a sperm in one
of the fat man's balls,

13:40.746 --> 13:42.868
so he couldn't get rid
of the fat man

13:42.937 --> 13:44.808
or any recent ancestor
on my father's side.

13:44.876 --> 13:47.048
Bertram had to go back
enough generations

13:47.115 --> 13:49.087
to make sure he
was snuffing out

13:49.155 --> 13:50.425
the ancestral source
of my genius

13:50.493 --> 13:52.028
without affecting his own.

13:52.097 --> 13:53.934
And apparently, it's
Leonardo da Vinci.

13:54.003 --> 13:56.476
DA VINCI:
Boy, I'm-a sick of da pizza.

13:56.545 --> 13:58.883
Well, we've gotta stop Bertram.
What are we gonna do?

13:58.951 --> 14:00.839
Don't worry, Brian,
I'll come up with something.

14:00.907 --> 14:02.044
Remember, I'm a genius.

14:02.110 --> 14:03.314
Like Thomas Edison.

14:03.365 --> 14:05.772
Thomas, what are
you doing?!

14:05.840 --> 14:07.477
Experimenting.

14:09.384 --> 14:10.920
Brian, look!

14:11.005 --> 14:12.475
(whirring, buzzing)

14:15.452 --> 14:17.157
Mama mia!
What is this-a?!

14:17.225 --> 14:19.732
Leonardo da Vinci, I presume.

14:19.798 --> 14:22.038
Who are you-a,
little-a lisping baby?

14:22.106 --> 14:25.114
Your assassin,
you overrated caricaturist!

14:28.675 --> 14:29.745
(gasps)
Stewie!

14:29.814 --> 14:31.315
That's my name,
don't wear it out.

14:31.384 --> 14:33.522
(laughs)

14:33.592 --> 14:34.944
See, that's
brand-new to him.

15:02.881 --> 15:05.537
(creaking)

15:39.325 --> 15:40.678
Ey!

15:40.746 --> 15:41.647
Ey!

16:17.759 --> 16:20.417
You're about to be yesterday's
hopscotch chalk

16:20.484 --> 16:22.406
on a sidewalk, Stewie!

16:22.457 --> 16:23.777
Erased!

16:23.828 --> 16:25.449
Yeah, no, I-I got
the metaphor,

16:25.500 --> 16:27.054
but, Bertram, if
you erase me,

16:27.121 --> 16:28.023
you'll destroy
the universe!

16:28.091 --> 16:29.511
What are you talking about?

16:29.597 --> 16:31.266
Listen to him, Bertram!
It's true!

16:31.317 --> 16:33.992
Brian and I were trapped outside
the space-time continuum

16:34.077 --> 16:35.831
and the only way for us

16:35.916 --> 16:38.506
to get back was to overload
my old return pad!

16:38.589 --> 16:40.796
The resulting explosion
was the big bang!

16:40.847 --> 16:43.002
So if you kill me,
you're killing yourself

16:43.053 --> 16:45.342
and everything else that ever
existed or will exist!

16:47.317 --> 16:48.519
Worth it!

16:48.588 --> 16:50.040
(groans)

16:52.416 --> 16:54.305
You fool!

17:12.275 --> 17:13.746
(grunts)

17:15.737 --> 17:17.040
Hey, Bertram,

17:17.109 --> 17:18.979
what's your favorite kind
of bottled water?

17:19.048 --> 17:20.082
Huh?

17:20.150 --> 17:21.754
Mine's Arrowhead.

17:21.805 --> 17:23.944
(groans)

17:26.134 --> 17:28.708
Wow, that was...

17:28.776 --> 17:29.897
dramatic.

17:32.990 --> 17:35.445
My God, he's really dead.

17:35.515 --> 17:36.983
This is it.

17:37.052 --> 17:38.322
Yeah.

17:38.390 --> 17:41.397
But why hasn't the universe
ceased to exist?

17:41.464 --> 17:42.568
I don't know.

17:42.635 --> 17:44.190
Unless...

17:44.257 --> 17:46.263
there's still a chance
of me being born.

17:46.331 --> 17:47.799
But da Vinci is dead...

17:47.868 --> 17:49.655
Yes, but we're still here.

17:49.724 --> 17:52.130
So I must have done, or will do,

17:52.198 --> 17:54.338
something that will save
the universe.

17:54.405 --> 17:56.845
If I can just figure out
what that is.

17:56.913 --> 17:58.015
(sniffs)

17:58.083 --> 17:59.921
Oh! Bertram just
voided his bowels.

17:59.989 --> 18:01.359
That's it!

18:01.426 --> 18:04.535
The genetic material needed
for my creation is inside me.

18:04.603 --> 18:06.943
So I'll take da Vinci's place
and pass it on.

18:07.011 --> 18:09.117
Wait, how did you get
that from "bowels"? Huh?

18:09.184 --> 18:10.871
I'm just wondering how
you got that from...

18:10.939 --> 18:12.091
You know what?
I don't care.

18:12.159 --> 18:13.262
Will, will that
work, though?

18:13.330 --> 18:14.265
You taking
da Vinci's place?

18:14.332 --> 18:16.605
Only one way to find out.

18:16.673 --> 18:18.912
All right, I'm going
to send you back five minutes

18:18.981 --> 18:20.584
after we disappeared
in the time machine.

18:20.651 --> 18:21.789
If all goes well,

18:21.856 --> 18:23.995
the universe should be
intact this time.

18:24.063 --> 18:25.617
Good luck, old friend.

18:25.685 --> 18:28.508
Hopefully, I'll be leaving you
a future to return to.

18:28.576 --> 18:30.514
I'm gonna miss you, Stewie.

18:30.582 --> 18:31.685
Good luck.

18:50.476 --> 18:52.582
You did it, Stewie.

18:52.650 --> 18:54.287
You did it.

19:03.483 --> 19:04.854
(sighs)

19:04.922 --> 19:06.056
(knocking on door)

19:08.700 --> 19:10.202
Uh... can I help you?

19:10.270 --> 19:11.841
(Italian accent):
Are you Brian Griffin?

19:11.909 --> 19:13.045
Yes.

19:13.113 --> 19:15.002
I'm-a from the Vatican,
and this is for you.

19:15.069 --> 19:16.238
What is it?

19:16.306 --> 19:18.093
The Vatican has been
holding this note for you

19:18.162 --> 19:19.999
for about 500 years.

19:20.068 --> 19:23.076
It was left to us by
Leonardo da Vinci himself,

19:23.143 --> 19:25.450
with instructions
that it be delivered

19:25.517 --> 19:30.480
to you on this exact-a
date at this exact-a time.

19:30.549 --> 19:32.370
<i>STEWIE:
Dear Brian,</i>

19:32.438 --> 19:33.792
<i>It's been a week
since you left,</i>

19:33.859 --> 19:35.949
<i>and I'm pretty sure I've set
things right.</i>

19:36.017 --> 19:38.722
<i>So I've built a cryogenic
stasis device,</i>

19:38.791 --> 19:41.564
<i>and am placing myself inside
it, leaving instructions for it</i>

19:41.633 --> 19:43.003
<i>to be buried
in very specific coordinates</i>

19:43.070 --> 19:45.677
<i>in what will be future Quahog.</i>

19:45.745 --> 19:47.582
<i>If everything goes according
to plan,</i>

19:47.651 --> 19:49.623
<i>I will have spent
the next 500 years</i>

19:49.707 --> 19:51.462
<i>buried beneath our basement.</i>

19:51.546 --> 19:52.766
Oh, my God!

19:52.851 --> 19:55.925
(clears throat)
A tip is-a customary!

20:11.574 --> 20:12.678
(clunk)

20:20.050 --> 20:22.005
Stewie!

20:23.445 --> 20:24.563
(whoosh)

20:24.631 --> 20:26.869
Stewie! Stewie, you okay?!

20:26.938 --> 20:29.712
(high-pitched voice): You gotta
kiss him to wake him up.

20:29.798 --> 20:31.385
What?
I'm not gonna kiss you.

20:31.453 --> 20:33.791
Stewie can't hear you.
He's not awake.

20:33.859 --> 20:35.329
Only a kiss will wake him up.

20:35.398 --> 20:36.851
Well, I better get Meg.

20:36.918 --> 20:38.856
Oh, oh, oh, where am I?
Wha...?

20:38.924 --> 20:40.362
Stewie, what the hell happened?!

20:40.413 --> 20:42.351
Oh, well, shortly
after you left,

20:42.419 --> 20:43.588
da Vinci's girlfriend
showed up,

20:43.655 --> 20:45.527
so I injected her
with my DNA.

20:45.596 --> 20:46.966
You had sex?

20:47.032 --> 20:49.071
No, I put my DNA
inside her.

20:49.139 --> 20:50.258
Right. You had sex.

20:50.326 --> 20:51.696
No, what are
you not getting?

20:51.764 --> 20:53.937
I put a sample of my
DNA in a syringe

20:54.003 --> 20:55.441
and I injected her
on the staircase,

20:55.509 --> 20:56.462
the couch and
the balcony.

20:56.545 --> 20:58.162
Well, whatever
the case, thank God.

20:58.368 --> 20:59.736
Yeah, I know. Oh, hey,

20:59.804 --> 21:01.797
I brought you something
from da Vinci's workshop.

21:02.942 --> 21:04.480
<i>A candle?
I can get a candle now.</i>

21:05.685 --> 21:07.557
You couldn't have grabbed me
one of his original notebooks?

21:08.094 --> 21:09.447
You know, I didn't have
to bring you back anything.

21:09.515 --> 21:11.704
It's almost like
you didn't.

21:11.705 --> 22:12.705
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru